.TH MEMSPYD 8 "October 2026" "memspy 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
memspyd \- detect high-confidence fileless malware behavior with eBPF
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B memspyd
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B memspyd
loads observational eBPF probes, evaluates local detection rules, retains alerts for 30 days, and exposes a read-only Unix socket API. It does not block, kill, quarantine, inspect payload memory, or open a network listener.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --config " PATH"
Use an exact-path allowlist other than /etc/memspy/allowlist.conf.
.TP
.BI --database " PATH"
Use an alert database other than /var/lib/memspy/alerts.db.
.TP
.BI --socket " PATH"
Use a Unix socket other than /run/memspy/memspy.sock.
.TP
.B --foreground
Run in the foreground. This is the packaged default.
.TP
.B --version
Print the version.
.TP
.B --help
Print command help.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/memspy/allowlist.conf
One exact rule ID and absolute executable path per line.
.TP
.I /var/lib/memspy/alerts.db
Root-owned SQLite alert history, mode 0600.
.TP
.I /run/memspy/memspy.sock
Local read-only API, mode 0660 and group memspy-ui.
.SH STORAGE FAILURE
If the SQLite database cannot open, the service preserves it, continues bounded live detection in degraded health, and retries storage every 60 seconds.
.SH SECURITY
The service requires x86_64 Linux 5.4 or newer and readable kernel BTF at /sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux. Add only trusted desktop users to memspy-ui. Membership permits reading alert metadata from all monitored users.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR memspy-ui (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1)
